WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 248: Davy Crockett: The King of the Wild Frontier, the Bear Hunter in Congress, and the Alamo

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:17.760
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series journeys into the rugged Appalachian Wilderness, the Halls of Congress, and the sunbaked ramparts of the Texas frontier.

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:26.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the courageous, unvarnished life of the King of the Wild Frontier, David Crockett.

5
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:38.800
[George] Born in August 1786 in a rough-hewn log cabin along the Nolichucky River in the wilderness of eastern Tennessee, David Crockett entered a harsh world on the ragged edge of American civilization.

6
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.200
[George] He was the fifth of nine children in an impoverished pioneer family struggling to survive on the frontier.

7
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:58.800
[George] When his father's roadside tavern failed, 12-year-old Davy was bound out as an indentured drover to settle family debts, herding cattle 400 miles entirely on foot through the Appalachian forests to Virginia.

8
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:12.400
[George] Returning home years later, young Crockett recognized his lack of schooling, working for 18 months to buy out his own indenture and paying for six months of private tutoring to learn basic reading, writing, and arithmetic.

9
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:30.080
[George] Blessed with supernatural woodcraft, sharp frontier wit, and uncanny marksmanship with a long flintlock rifle, Crockett became a legendary hunter in the cane breaks of the Obian River, famously claiming to have killed 105 black bears in a single grueling hunting season.

10
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:47.760
[George] Modern laser scanning and archival research confirmed that Crockett was not merely a mythical backwoods braggart, but a hardened, self reliant woodsman who understood the deep ecology, seasonal migrations, and dangerous waterways of the early American frontier with intimate precision.

11
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:58.160
[George] In 1813, following the massacre of settlers at Fort Mims, Crockett enlisted as a scout in the Tennessee militia under General Andrew Jackson during the Creek War.

12
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:07.560
[George] Tracking tribal war parties through flooded Alabama swamps while developing an enduring, first-hand empathy for the tragic plight of displaced native peoples.

13
00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:22.120
[George] Crockett's formative years in the Appalachian backcountry taught him that survival depended on unyielding loyalty to one's neighbors, an ethic of mutual assistance in cabin raisings and corn huskings that shaped his egalitarian political philosophy.

14
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:33.640
[George] In the 1820s, Crockett's reputation for transparent honesty, generous common sense, and captivating backwoods storytelling propelled him rapidly into Tennessee public life.

15
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:48.200
[George] Serving first as a rural county magistrate and town commissioner, he was elected to the Tennessee state legislature, where he championed the rights of poor pioneer squatters against wealthy land speculators who held fraudulent colonial land warrants.

16
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:57.960
[George] In 1827, Crockett was elected to the United States House of Representatives, arriving in the muddy capital of Washington dressed in homespun wool.

17
00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:12.840
[George] Eastern newspapers were captivated by his colorful colloquial expressions, while popular Broadway stage plays like The Lion of the West transformed him into an overnight national sensation as the mythical folk hero Colonel Nimrod Wildfire.

18
00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:22.280
[George] Yet behind his self-deprecating frontier humor lay an intensely principled legislator who refused to be controlled by party bosses or special interests.

19
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:27.960
[George] Crockett regularly walked the halls of Congress demanding land reform for impoverished settlers.

20
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:38.760
[George] Warning that an aristocratic class of eastern bankers and speculators was conspiring to turn Western pioneers into feudal tenants upon the very soil they had cleared with their bare hands.

21
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:48.720
[George] His speeches were noted for their biting satirical wit and earthy parables, disarming polished eastern orators with simple home truths that resonated across the Republic.

22
00:03:48.960 --> 00:04:02.960
[George] During his terms in Washington, Crockett was a frequent guest of Eastern literary societies, where his unvarnished frontiersman perspective provided an earthy, democratic antidote to the formal diplomatic protocol of the Capitol.

23
00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:17.280
[George] The defining moral test of Crockett's political life arrived in the spring of 1830, when President Andrew Jackson, the supreme political titan and unquestioned kingmaker of Tennessee, demanded the passage of the Indian Removal Act.

24
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:34.720
[George] Jackson's legislation proposed the forced military expulsion of over 60,000 civilized Native Americans from the Cherokee, Creek, Choctaw, Chickasaw, and Seminole nations from their ancestral Southern homelands across the Mississippi River into the dry barrens of Oklahoma.

25
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:45.200
[George] While other Southern politicians cravenly fell into line to seize fertile native cotton lands, David Crockett took the floor of the House of Representatives in passionate opposition.

26
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:56.960
[George] Standing practically alone among Southern lawmakers, Crockett denounced the bill as a monstrous act of dishonor, treaty violation, and institutional theft against a peaceful and sovereign people.

27
00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:08.560
[George] Crockett boldly declared that even if his vote destroyed him politically, he would never violate his conscience or vote for a measure that bowed the Republic's head in eternal shame before Almighty God.

28
00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:18.080
[George] Andrew Jackson was furious, vowing to eradicate Crockett from public life and pouring massive party funds into Tennessee to back rival candidates.

29
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:30.040
[George] Crockett was narrowly defeated in 1831, fought his way back to Congress in 1833, but was targeted again by Jackson's ruthless machine and defeated in 1835.

30
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:35.560
[George] Disgusted by political venality, Crockett delivered his famous farewell to his constituents.

31
00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:43.320
[George] I told them that I was going to serve them as honestly as I had done, but if they did not elect me, they might all go to hell.

32
00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.160
[George] And I will go to Texas.

33
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:58.520
[George] Crockett's passionate floor speeches against the removal of the Cherokees invoked the memory of native warriors who had fought alongside him in the Creek War, reminding Congress that national honor cannot be sacrificed for territorial greed.

34
00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:16.680
[George] In November 1835, packing his favorite 40-caliber flintlock rifle, Old Betsy, and accompanied by a small band of loyal Tennessee volunteers, 49-year-old David Crockett turned his back on the United States and rode southwest toward the frontier of Mexican Texas.

35
00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:32.360
[George] The Mexican province was in open, armed rebellion against the centralist military dictatorship of General Antonio Lopez de Santa Ana, who had abolished the liberal Mexican Constitution of 1824 and declared himself the supreme master of the Republic.

36
00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:45.000
[George] As Crockett traveled through Arkansas and crossed the Red River, hundreds of settlers turned out in frontier hamlets to toast the famous Bear Hunter and Congressman, singing songs and drinking whiskey in his honor.

37
00:06:45.000 --> 00:07:01.160
[George] Arriving in Nacadoches in January 1836, Crockett signed the Oath of Allegiance to the Provisional Government of Texas, famously insisting on inserting the word Republican into the document to ensure he was pledging himself only to a free constitutional republic.

38
00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:17.200
[George] In early February, Crockett and a dozen Tennessee sharpshooters rode into San Antonio de Bakesar, joining a garrison of 189 volunteers garrisoned in the crumbling adobe and limestone walls of the former Spanish Catholic mission known as the Alamo.

39
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:28.000
[George] Crockett immediately refused any military rank or command title, stating simply that he had come as a private citizen and volunteer to fight alongside free men for liberty.

40
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:41.680
[George] As Crockett and his volunteers crossed the rolling prairies of Texas, they were greeted by settlers who recognized the former congressmen, singing folk ballads and providing fresh horses to speed their journey toward San Antonio.

41
00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:59.440
[George] On February 23, 1836, the vanguard of Santa Ana's massive army of 2,000 Mexican soldiers arrived in San Antonio, raising the ominous blood red flag of no quarter from the bell tower of San Fernando Church to signal that no prisoners would be spared.

42
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:07.920
[George] Inside the Alamo, young Lieutenant Colonel William Barrett Travis and the knife-fighting legend Jim Bowie prepared for a hopeless defense.

43
00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:18.800
[George] For thirteen freezing days, while Mexican heavy artillery battered the cracked mission walls night and day, Crockett served as the emotional and spiritual anchor of the garrison.

44
00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:32.960
[George] Holding his position at the low wooden palisade connecting the church to the long barracks, Crockett directed deadly rifle fire against Mexican gunners, picking off artillerymen at unbelievable distances of 200 yards with old Betsy.

45
00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:50.400
[George] At night, amid freezing sleet and exploding mortar shells, Crockett pulled out his wooden fiddle and played lively frontier jigs, engaging in hilarious musical duels with Scottish bagpiper John McGregor to lift the morale of exhausted men who knew that no reinforcements were coming.

46
00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Travis praised Crockett in his desperate letters to the outside world, noting that the former congressman was always the first to animate the men and the last to seek shelter.

47
00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:15.080
[George] Eyewitness accounts from inside the besieged Alamo describe Crockett as an indefatigable sentinel, walking the palisades in his buckskin coat, joking with the wounded, and keeping spirits high under relentless round-the-clock bombardment.

48
00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:30.280
[George] At 5:30 on the pitch-black, freezing morning of Sunday, March 6, 1836, the eerie notes of the Mexican bugles sounded the terrifying call of the El de Guelo, the ancient Spanish death march decreeing total annihilation.

49
00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:37.320
[George] Two thousand Mexican infantrymen surged forward in four charging columns with ladders, axes and muskets.

50
00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.760
[George] Travis fell dead on the north battery in the opening minutes.

51
00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.800
[George] Bowie was slaughtered on his cot in the low barracks.

52
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:57.320
[George] Crockett and his Tennessee volunteers stood their ground at the low palisade in front of the church chapel, pouring a murderous hail of rifle fire into the charging Mexican columns until their black powder and lead balls were completely spent.

53
00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:06.040
[George] Clubbing their empty muskets like giant war hammers, Crockett and his men fought hand to hand against Mexican bayonets in the smoke-filled courtyard.

54
00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:21.480
[George] Eyewitness accounts from Mexican officers like Lieutenant Jose Enrique de la Peña record that Crockett was among a handful of survivors cornered in the outer ruins, their clothes shredded by musket balls, surrounded by mounds of fallen Mexican infantry.

55
00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:35.400
[George] When General Manuel Castrelan presented the courageous prisoners to Santa Ana, the ruthless dictator furiously rebuked his officers for taking captives and ordered his staff officers to execute them immediately with drawn sabres.

56
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:50.800
[George] The furious final defense of the Alamo Chapel courtyard was fought in near-total darkness, illuminated only by the flashes of musket fire and burning timber, as Crockett and his riflemen fought to their last breath with clubbed rifles and frontier hunting knives.

57
00:10:51.120 --> 00:11:05.040
[George] Santa Ana ordered the bodies of Crockett, Travis, Bowie, and all 189 Alamo defenders dragged into great heaps and burned to ashes on pyres of wood, hoping to erase their memory from the face of the earth.

58
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:09.440
[George] But Santa Ana's savagery ignited an uncontrollable firestorm.

59
00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:22.880
[George] Six weeks later, General Sam Houston's Texan army charged at San Jacinto with the thunderous battle cry, Remember the Alamo, routing the Mexican forces in 18 minutes and securing Texas independence.

60
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:28.560
[George] In the centuries since, David Crockett has ascended into the pantheon of global folklore.

61
00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:52.560
[George] While television shows and movies popularized his coonskin cap and bear hunting legends, the real historical David Crockett remains an extraordinary exemplar of American character, a humble, uncorrupted frontiersman who loved liberty more than power, who stood alone against an unjust president to protect the human rights of Native Americans, and who died on his feet fighting for freedom.

62
00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:57.520
[George] His personal life motto remains carved into the conscience of the American nation.

63
00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:00.720
[George] Be always sure you are right, then go ahead.

64
00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:05.680
[George] Crockett's enduring martyrdom became the foundation stone of Texas independence.

65
00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:14.640
[George] Transforming a backwoods bear hunter from Tennessee into an immortal international symbol of heroic defiance against overwhelming tyranny.

66
00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:18.520
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

67
00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:32.040
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non-president American series continues with episode 249: Meriwether Lewis and William Clark, The Core of Discovery, the Unknown West, and the Pacific Ocean.

68
00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.600
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

